+Rank-ordered PLE tables use buffered positional reads on Windows. They serve
+without a background table worker; load-fill and prewarm stay disabled. The
+same encoded table bytes, embedded rank digest and row checks are retained.
+Windows host allocation sizing reserves 18 GiB from both available physical RAM
+and the allocating process's available commit, then takes their minimum. Device sizing
+can use firmware-dedicated memory only with exact HIP R0600 LUID/AMD DXGI node-0
+identity and a valid adapter-global PDH `Dedicated Usage` sample. Free bytes
+are capped by HIP free, descriptor dedicated capacity minus global usage, and
+LOCAL process budget minus process usage; shared system memory is never added.
+HIP node mask 1 identifies node 0. A zero mask is accepted only for the
+qualified gfx1151 integrated single-visible-device tuple: HIP 7.2 leaves this
+field empty, and a real allocation was observed in the matching LOCAL node-0
+and PDH phys-0 counters. Other missing or unsupported identity/counter data
+fall back to the reserved host allowance. Zero host allowance admits no device
+capacity. These volatile snapshots do not guarantee residency or allocation
+success; keep a host reserve guard during model loading. The explicit HIP R0600
+x64 architecture query refuses failed or malformed architecture data.
+This source path still needs native GPU/model qualification; a build alone
+does not establish runtime correctness or throughput.
+
 The Windows build does not imply support for Unix-only PLE conversion,
-hot-row/direct-read or hot-pack I/O, cache-residency probing, or prewarm paths;
+separate hot-row/direct-read or hot-pack I/O, cache-residency probing, or prewarm;
 those I/O entry points report unsupported on Windows. POSIX-FD VMM sharing,
 Redline, and VMM KV are not native Windows capabilities.
 
